// FOUNDATION — every card has a concept, worked calculation and two distinct question tracks.
add("scalars-coordinates","Scalars, coordinates & dimensions",0,"Beginner","both",
"A scalar measures one quantity; a vector needs a coordinate system to describe a direction. Deep learning tensors attach meaning to each axis — samples, features, heads or channels.",
"scalar c ∈ ℝ ; vector x ∈ ℝⁿ ; matrix A ∈ ℝᵐˣⁿ",
"A batch has 32 samples and 128 features. What is its shape?","32 × 128, because each row is one sample and each column is a feature.",
"State the order of a matrix with 4 rows and 3 columns.","4 × 3; it has 12 entries.",
"What does X ∈ ℝ³²ˣ¹²⁸ mean in a neural network?","32 observations, each with 128 numerical features.",
"A vector is not defined by its array of numbers alone: its chosen basis matters.");
add("vectors-geometry","Vectors as arrows & data",0,"Beginner","both",
"A vector is simultaneously an arrow, a list of coordinates and an element of an abstract vector space. Addition moves arrows; scalar multiplication stretches them.",
"u + v = (u₁+v₁, u₂+v₂);  c·u = (cu₁, cu₂)",
"Add u=(2,−1) and v=(3,4).","u+v=(5,3). Geometrically, move v to the tip of u.",
"Find 3(1,−2)+2(−1,3).","(3,−6)+(−2,6)=(1,0).",
"Why can a word embedding be treated as a vector?","It stores coordinates in a learned feature space where linear operations can encode relationships.",
"Coordinates can change under a new basis although the geometric vector does not.");
add("vector-products","Dot products & angles",0,"Beginner","both",
"The dot product measures alignment. Zero means perpendicular, not that one vector has zero length. In machine learning it is a basic similarity score and the core of an attention logit.",
"u·v = Σᵢuᵢvᵢ = ‖u‖‖v‖ cos θ",
"Compute (1,2)·(3,−1).","3−2=1. The positive result means an acute angle.",
"Are (2,1) and (1,−2) orthogonal?","Yes: 2×1+1×(−2)=0.",
"For a query q=(1,2), compare keys k₁=(2,1), k₂=(−1,1).","q·k₁=4 and q·k₂=1; the unnormalized match to k₁ is stronger.",
"Large dot products can come from large norms as well as alignment.");
add("norms-metrics","Norms, distances & similarity",0,"Beginner","both",
"A norm assigns a length; a metric assigns distance between objects. L₂ distances capture geometric closeness while cosine similarity removes magnitude.",
"‖x‖₂ = √(Σᵢxᵢ²); d(x,y)=‖x−y‖₂",
"Find ‖(3,4)‖₂.","√(9+16)=5.",
"Calculate the Euclidean distance from (1,2) to (4,6).","√(3²+4²)=5.",
"Why normalize image or sentence embeddings before cosine similarity?","Normalization removes length effects; normalized dot product equals cosine similarity.",
"An L₁ norm and an L₂ norm solve different optimization problems.");
add("matrix-transformations","Matrices as transformations",0,"Beginner","both",
"A matrix is a function that moves every vector linearly. Its columns tell you where the basis vectors land; this turns abstract multiplication into visible geometry.",
"A[x,y]ᵀ = x·column₁(A) + y·column₂(A)",
"Let A=[[2,0],[0,1]]. Transform (1,3).","A(1,3)=(2,3): a horizontal stretch of 2.",
"Find the image of e₁=(1,0) under [[1,2],[3,4]].","The first column (1,3).",
"A weight layer maps 3 input features to 5 outputs. What is W's shape?","For y=Wx with x a column vector, W is 5×3.",
"Matrix multiplication is not elementwise multiplication.");
add("matrix-multiplication","Matrix multiplication & composition",0,"Beginner","both",
"Composition is order-sensitive: do B first and then A to obtain AB. Every hidden neural network layer composes functions, making matrix multiplication the backbone of inference.",
"(AB)ᵢⱼ = Σₖ AᵢₖBₖⱼ ; (AB)C=A(BC)",
"Multiply [[1,2],[0,1]] by [3,4]ᵀ.","Output [11,4]ᵀ.",
"Is AB always equal to BA?","No. E.g., a stretch followed by a rotation generally differs from the reverse order.",
"If X has shape (32,128) and W has (128,64), what is XW?","A (32,64) batch of hidden features.",
"AB and BA may have different shapes, and may not both be defined.");
add("determinant-geometry","Determinants & oriented area",0,"Beginner","both",
"The determinant is the signed scaling of area (or volume) by a transformation. A zero determinant means information was crushed into fewer dimensions.",
"det[[a,b],[c,d]] = ad − bc",
"Find det([[2,1],[3,4]]).","2×4−1×3=5: areas grow by a factor of 5, preserving orientation.",
"For [[1,2],[2,4]], find det and comment on invertibility.","1×4−2×2=0; the matrix is singular.",
"What does det(A)=0 imply about a dense layer?","Its linear map loses at least one dimension; a nonzero input can map to zero.",
"Absolute determinant gives area scale; the sign carries orientation.");
add("linear-combinations","Span, combinations & reachability",0,"Beginner","both",
"A span is everything you can build by mixing given vectors. Linear systems ask if the target vector lies inside the span of the columns.",
"span(v₁,…,vₖ) = {Σᵢcᵢvᵢ}",
"Can (4,6) be obtained from (2,3)?","Yes: (4,6)=2(2,3).",
"Do (1,0) and (0,1) span ℝ²?","Yes: (a,b)=a(1,0)+b(0,1).",
"Why is an embedding basis useful?","Its span determines which feature directions a linear readout can express.",
"More vectors do not necessarily produce a larger span.");

// UNIT III — aligned to topics recalled from IPU course materials.
add("gauss-elimination","Gaussian elimination",1,"Intermediate","ipu",
"Elimination is structured bookkeeping: use one equation to remove an unknown from another. Computationally it is an algorithm for turning a hard system into triangular form.",
"Rⱼ ← Rⱼ − c·Rᵢ",
"Solve x+y=5 and 2x+y=8.","Subtract the first equation from the second: x=3, then y=2.",
"Solve x+2y=7, 3x−y=7 by elimination.","Multiply the first by 3 and subtract: 7y=14 → y=2, x=3.",
"Why is solving Ax=b numerically preferable to explicitly forming A⁻¹b?","Stable factorization/solve routines avoid unnecessary inverse computation and numerical error.",
"Swapping, scaling by nonzero constants and row replacement preserve the solution set.");
add("echelon-rref","Row echelon form & RREF",1,"Intermediate","ipu",
"Echelon form reveals pivots like steps on a staircase. The reduced form isolates pivot variables and makes free variables visible.",
"REF: leading positions move right; RREF: pivots = 1 and pivot columns otherwise 0",
"Reduce [[1,2],[2,4]] by R₂←R₂−2R₁.","REF=[[1,2],[0,0]]; pivot count 1.",
"Find the RREF of [[1,2],[2,5]].","[[1,0],[0,1]] after elimination and clearing the upper-right entry.",
"How does pivot counting help identify redundant learned features?","Fewer pivots than columns means some feature directions are exact linear combinations.",
"Echelon form is not unique, but RREF is unique.");
add("rank-nullity","Rank, null space & normal form",1,"Intermediate","ipu",
"Rank counts independent output directions. Nullity counts input directions that disappear. Together they describe exactly what a linear map preserves or loses.",
"rank(A)+nullity(A)=number of columns",
"For A=[[1,2],[2,4]], find rank and nullity.","rank=1 because row 2 is twice row 1; nullity=2−1=1.",
"Find rank of [[1,2,3],[2,4,6],[0,1,1]].","2, since one row is dependent and two pivot rows remain.",
"What does rank deficiency mean for a feature matrix?","Some features are redundant; the linear predictor cannot identify unique coefficients without additional constraints.",
"Rank is pivot count, not the number of nonzero entries.");
add("system-consistency","Consistency of linear systems",1,"Intermediate","ipu",
"Whether Ax=b can be solved depends on where b lives relative to column space. Augmenting b to A exposes the crucial rank comparison.",
"consistent ⇔ rank(A)=rank([A|b])",
"Consider x+y=2 and 2x+2y=5.","The second equation contradicts double the first. rank(A)=1, augmented rank=2: inconsistent.",
"State the condition for infinitely many solutions for n unknowns.","rank(A)=rank([A|b])<n.",
"Why can least squares be needed in real datasets?","No exact weight vector may satisfy every training constraint; minimizing residuals replaces exact consistency.",
"Rank(A)<n alone does not guarantee consistency.");
add("linear-independence","Linear independence & dependence",1,"Intermediate","ipu",
"Independent vectors contribute genuinely new directions. Dependence means at least one vector can be made from the others.",
"Σᵢcᵢvᵢ=0 has only trivial solution ⇔ independent",
"Are (1,0),(0,1),(1,1) independent in ℝ²?","No: the third equals the sum of the first two.",
"Test (1,2) and (2,4) for independence.","Dependent: the second equals 2 times the first.",
"Why does correlated data challenge linear regression?","Nearly dependent columns make weight estimation unstable; regularization can help.",
"Zero vector in any set makes the set dependent.");
add("vector-spaces","Vector spaces & subspaces",1,"Intermediate","ipu",
"A vector space is a set closed under addition and scalar multiplication. Subspaces let us reason about signals, solutions and representations without choosing coordinates first.",
"U ⊆ V is a subspace if 0∈U and au+bv∈U",
"Is {(x,y):x+y=0} a subspace of ℝ²?","Yes: it contains zero and is closed under linear combinations.",
"Is {(x,y):x+y=1} a subspace?","No: it does not contain the zero vector.",
"Give an example of a neural-network-related subspace.","The column space of a data matrix or the null space of a weight matrix.",
"Passing through the origin is necessary for a linear subspace.");
add("basis-dimension","Basis, dimension & coordinates",1,"Intermediate","ipu",
"A basis is a minimal vocabulary of directions: enough to describe every vector, with no redundancy. Dimension is the size of any basis.",
"v = Σᵢcᵢbᵢ ; dim(V)=#basis vectors",
"Does {(1,0),(0,1)} form a basis for ℝ²?","Yes: independent and spanning.",
"Find a basis of span{(1,2),(2,4)}.","{(1,2)}. Its dimension is 1.",
"Why are low-dimensional latent spaces useful?","They compress information into fewer independent coordinates.",
"A set can span without being a basis if it contains redundant vectors.");
add("inverse-cramer","Inverse matrices & Cramer's rule",1,"Intermediate","ipu",
"An inverse undoes an invertible transformation. Cramer's rule describes solutions via determinants, useful for exam algebra but inefficient for large real-world systems.",
"A⁻¹A=I ; xᵢ=det(Aᵢ)/det(A), det(A)≠0",
"For A=[[2,0],[0,4]], find A⁻¹.","[[1/2,0],[0,1/4]].",
"Solve 2x+y=5, x−y=1 by Cramer's rule.","det A=−3; x=2, y=1.",
"Why do libraries use torch.linalg.solve rather than inverse for Ax=b?","Direct solve is generally more stable and efficient than forming an explicit inverse.",
"An inverse only exists for square, full-rank matrices.");
add("linear-maps","Linear transformations & kernels",1,"Intermediate","both",
"Linearity means a function respects superposition. Kernels contain invisible directions; images contain reachable outputs — two sides of the same idea.",
"T(au+bv)=aT(u)+bT(v)",
"Is T(x,y)=(2x,3y) linear?","Yes, represented by diag(2,3).",
"Is T(x,y)=(x+1,y) linear?","No: T(0,0)≠0.",
"Is a dense layer y=Wx+b linear when b≠0?","Strictly, no. It is an affine transformation.",
"The word linear is often used informally for affine neural layers.");
add("special-matrices","Symmetric, skew & orthogonal matrices",1,"Intermediate","ipu",
"Structure gives matrix operations stronger guarantees. Symmetry mirrors across a diagonal; orthogonality preserves length and inner products.",
"Aᵀ=A ; Aᵀ=−A ; QᵀQ=I",
"Classify [[0,2],[−2,0]].","Skew-symmetric because its transpose equals its negative.",
"Verify that [[0,−1],[1,0]] is orthogonal.","QᵀQ=I; it rotates 90° while preserving lengths.",
"Why are orthogonal initializations used in some neural networks?","They can help preserve signal magnitudes and manage gradient flow.",
"Orthogonal matrices need not be symmetric.");
add("eigenvectors","Eigenvalues & eigenvectors",1,"Intermediate","both",
"An eigenvector is a direction a matrix does not turn; only its length or sign changes. Eigenvalues quantify scaling on these special directions.",
"Av=λv ; det(A−λI)=0",
"For diag(2,3), identify eigenvalues.","λ₁=2, λ₂=3; coordinate axes are eigenvectors.",
"Find eigenvalues of [[4,0],[0,−1]].","4 and −1.",
"How can eigenvalues explain exploding or vanishing repeated linear dynamics?","Iterating A multiplies components along eigenvectors roughly by λᵏ, growing when |λ|>1 and shrinking when |λ|<1.",
"An arbitrary vector is generally not an eigenvector.");
add("diagonalization","Diagonalization & eigenbasis",1,"Advanced","ipu",
"Diagonalization is a change of coordinates that makes a transformation act independently on each basis direction. It simplifies powers and linear dynamics.",
"A=PDP⁻¹ ⇒ Aᵏ=PDᵏP⁻¹",
"For A=diag(2,3), compute A³.","diag(8,27).",
"Give the condition for an n×n matrix to be diagonalizable.","It must have n linearly independent eigenvectors.",
"Why do spectral decompositions simplify graph diffusion or covariance analysis?","Each eigenmode evolves separately, converting coupled operations into scalar multiplications.",
"Repeated eigenvalues do not automatically prevent diagonalization.");
add("quadratic-forms","Quadratic forms & definiteness",1,"Advanced","both",
"A quadratic form measures a direction-dependent curvature or energy. Positive definiteness is the bridge from matrix theory to stable optimization.",
"q(x)=xᵀAx ; A≻0 ⇔ xᵀAx>0 for x≠0",
"For A=diag(2,3), q(1,2)=?","2×1²+3×2²=14.",
"Classify x²+y² and x²−y².","First is positive definite; second is indefinite.",
"How is a positive-definite Hessian related to a local minimum?","At a stationary point, positive-definite Hessian is a sufficient second-order condition for a strict local minimum.",
"Positive semidefinite allows zero curvature in some directions.");

// DEEPER GEOMETRY
add("orthogonality-projection","Orthogonality & projection",2,"Intermediate","both",
"Projection finds the closest point in a subspace. It is the geometric idea behind least squares, feature decomposition and gradient components.",
"projᵤ(v) = ((v·u)/(u·u))u",
"Project v=(3,4) onto the x-axis.","(3,0).",
"Project (2,2) onto span{(1,0)}.","(2,0).",
"Why is orthogonal projection useful for data fitting?","The residual is perpendicular to the prediction subspace at the least-squares optimum.",
"Never divide by ‖u‖² when u=0.");
add("least-squares","Least squares & pseudoinverse",2,"Advanced","both",
"When equations contradict, choose the answer with the smallest squared residual. This is the geometric form of linear regression.",
"minₓ ‖Ax−b‖² ; AᵀAx=Aᵀb",
"Fit a constant c to observations 2,4,6 by least squares.","c equals their mean, 4.",
"Find best constant prediction for y=(1,3,5).","c=3.",
"What is the role of XᵀX in regression, and when is it troublesome?","It appears in normal equations; ill-conditioning and collinearity can make it unstable. QR/SVD often help.",
"Do not blindly invert XᵀX when rank is deficient.");
add("change-of-basis","Change of basis & coordinate systems",2,"Intermediate","both",
"The same vector can have multiple coordinate descriptions. A basis change transforms descriptions, not the underlying geometry.",
"[v]ᴮ = P⁻¹[v]ˢᵗᵈ",
"If B={(2,0),(0,1)}, find coordinates of (4,3).","(2,3) in basis B.",
"Express (6,2) in basis {(3,0),(0,1)}.","Coordinates (2,2).",
"Why can a linear feature representation be rotated without losing information?","An invertible change of basis preserves all information, though individual feature coordinates change.",
"Transform both vectors and operators consistently when changing basis.");
add("spectral-theorem","Spectral theorem for symmetric matrices",2,"Advanced","both",
"Real symmetric matrices have orthonormal eigenvectors and real eigenvalues. This converts covariance and curvature into interpretable principal directions.",
"A=Aᵀ ⇒ A=QΛQᵀ with QᵀQ=I",
"For diag(3,5), identify Q and Λ.","Q=I and Λ=diag(3,5).",
"Are symmetric real matrices guaranteed diagonalizable?","Yes, by an orthogonal matrix.",
"Why does PCA rely on symmetric covariance matrices?","Their eigenvectors define orthogonal principal directions and eigenvalues measure variance along them.",
"Eigenvalues of a general real matrix need not be real.");
add("svd-pca","SVD & principal component analysis",2,"Advanced","dl",
"SVD decomposes any matrix into two rotations (or orthogonal changes of basis) and axis-aligned scaling. The leading singular directions carry the most energy in low-rank approximation.",
"A=UΣVᵀ ; Aₖ=Σᵢ₌₁ᵏσᵢuᵢvᵢᵀ",
"A has singular values 5 and 1. Which rank-1 approximation wins?","Keep the σ=5 component; its lost squared Frobenius energy is 1.",
"What is the rank of diag(4,0,0)?","1; only one nonzero singular value.",
"Why does truncated SVD compress embeddings or model weights?","Keeping a few dominant singular components reduces dimension while minimizing Frobenius reconstruction error at fixed rank.",
"Uncentered SVD of data is not identical to PCA on centered data.");
add("numerical-conditioning","Conditioning & floating-point stability",2,"Advanced","dl",
"A nearly singular matrix amplifies input and rounding errors. Numerical linear algebra is about accurate computation, not just exact symbolic formulas.",
"κ₂(A)=σmax(A)/σmin(A)",
"For diag(100,1), compute κ₂.","100.",
"Why is det(A) alone a poor numerical conditioning test?","Determinant depends on scale and dimension; a nonzero determinant can coexist with severe ill-conditioning.",
"Why does a poorly conditioned design matrix slow or destabilize optimization?","Some directions respond much more strongly than others; step sizes suitable for one direction are ineffective in another.",
"Small determinant does not by itself mean poor conditioning.");
add("covariance-psd","Covariance, PSD & whitening",2,"Advanced","dl",
"Covariance summarizes how features vary together; its eigenvectors locate principal directions and its eigenvalues give variance. Whitening rescales them toward equal variance.",
"C=(1/n)XᵀX (for centered X) ; C⪰0",
"If centered data have C=diag(9,1), which axis varies more?","The first axis has variance 9 vs 1.",
"Can a covariance matrix have a negative eigenvalue?","Not mathematically, because covariance matrices are positive semidefinite.",
"Why do whitening or normalization layers affect training?","Rescaling and decorrelation can change conditioning and the geometry of optimization.",
"Always center data when computing standard covariance.");

// IPU UNIT IV — full mapped topic list
add("scalar-vector-fields","Scalar & vector fields",3,"Beginner","ipu",
"A scalar field assigns a number to every point (temperature); a vector field assigns an arrow (wind velocity). This language turns multivariable calculus into geometry.",
"f:ℝ³→ℝ ; F:ℝ³→ℝ³",
"Classify f(x,y)=x²+y² and F(x,y)=(−y,x).","f is scalar; F is vector.",
"Give one scalar and one vector field in mechanics.","Temperature T(x,y,z), velocity v(x,y,z).",
"Which field describes the gradient of a scalar neural loss over parameters?","The gradient is a vector field: one gradient vector for each parameter point.",
"Field arrows and field values are functions of position.");
add("partial-derivatives","Partial derivatives & total differential",3,"Beginner","both",
"A partial derivative changes one variable while freezing others. The total differential approximates all small changes together.",
"df = (∂f/∂x)dx+(∂f/∂y)dy",
"For f=x²y, compute ∂f/∂x.","2xy, treating y as constant.",
"For f=x²y+3y², find ∂f/∂y.","x²+6y.",
"What does a partial derivative of loss with respect to one weight mean?","The instantaneous loss sensitivity to that weight while holding other independent weights fixed.",
"Partial and total derivatives are not interchangeable.");
add("vector-functions","Vector-valued functions & derivatives",3,"Intermediate","ipu",
"Curves are vector-valued functions of a parameter, usually time. Their derivative is a tangent/velocity vector, and the second derivative is acceleration.",
"r(t)=(x(t),y(t),z(t)); v(t)=r′(t)",
"For r(t)=(t,t²,0), find v(2).","r′(t)=(1,2t,0); v(2)=(1,4,0).",
"Find r′(t) for r=(sin t, cos t, t).","(cos t,−sin t,1).",
"How is a parameter trajectory relevant to optimization?","Gradient descent generates a discrete path θ₀,θ₁,… through parameter space.",
"Differentiate each coordinate with respect to the parameter.");
add("arc-length","Arc length & unit tangent",3,"Intermediate","ipu",
"Speed is the length of the velocity vector; integrating speed gives distance traveled along a curve — not displacement.",
"L=∫ₐᵇ‖r′(t)‖dt ; T=r′/‖r′‖",
"For r(t)=(3t,4t), t∈[0,2], find arc length.","Speed=5, so L=∫₀²5dt=10.",
"Find length of r(t)=(t,0), 0≤t≤3.","3.",
"How might arc length describe a training path?","Σ‖θₖ₊₁−θₖ‖ measures distance traveled in parameter space, even if the model returns near its starting point.",
"Arc length is the integral of speed, not the integral of coordinates.");
add("curvature-torsion","Curvature & torsion",3,"Advanced","ipu",
"Curvature measures how rapidly a curve bends; torsion measures how it twists out of its osculating plane. A straight line has curvature zero.",
"κ=‖r′×r″‖/‖r′‖³ ; τ=((r′×r″)·r‴)/‖r′×r″‖²",
"A circle has radius 2. What is its curvature?","Constant κ=1/2.",
"State the curvature of a circle of radius R.","1/R (for R>0).",
"What could high curvature of an optimization trajectory indicate?","Updates repeatedly change direction; it may signal challenging geometry, oscillation or sharp changes.",
"Torsion requires a 3D regular curve with nonzero curvature.");
add("directional-gradient","Gradient & directional derivative",3,"Intermediate","both",
"The gradient points toward greatest instantaneous increase. A directional derivative is the projection of that gradient onto a unit direction.",
"Dᵤf = ∇f·u ; ∇f=(fₓ,fᵧ,f_z)",
"For f=x²+y², find ∇f at (1,2).","(2,4).",
"Find Dᵤf at (1,2) for f=x²+y², u=(1,0).","2.",
"Why is −∇L the local steepest-descent direction in Euclidean geometry?","For unit u, ∇L·u is minimal when u points opposite the gradient by Cauchy–Schwarz.",
"Directional derivative formula assumes u is a unit vector.");
add("divergence-curl","Divergence & curl",3,"Intermediate","ipu",
"Divergence measures local outflow; curl measures local rotation. They are different ways of differentiating vector fields.",
"div F=∂F₁/∂x+∂F₂/∂y+∂F₃/∂z ; curl F=∇×F",
"For F=(x,y,z), find divergence.","1+1+1=3.",
"Find div(x²,y²,z²).","2x+2y+2z.",
"What is the difference between divergence and a neural network gradient?","Gradient maps scalar→vector; divergence maps vector→scalar.",
"Zero divergence does not imply zero curl.");
add("line-integrals","Line integrals & work",3,"Intermediate","ipu",
"A line integral adds contributions along a path. Integrating a force field dot tangent displacement gives work done by the field.",
"∫꜀F·dr = ∫ₐᵇF(r(t))·r′(t)dt",
"For F=(1,0), move along r(t)=(t,0), 0≤t≤2.","Work=∫₀²1dt=2.",
"Evaluate ∫꜀(2,0)·dr from (0,0) to (3,0).","6.",
"How could an integral along a weight-space path be interpreted?","For a conservative gradient field it equals the change in loss between endpoints.",
"A scalar line integral ∫f ds differs from a vector work integral ∫F·dr.");
add("conservative-path","Path independence & potentials",3,"Advanced","ipu",
"A conservative field is the gradient of a scalar potential. Its work between points depends only on endpoints, not the chosen path.",
"F=∇φ ⇒ ∫꜀F·dr=φ(B)−φ(A)",
"For φ=x²+y², compute work (0,0)→(1,2).","φ(1,2)−φ(0,0)=5.",
"Find a potential for F=(2x,2y).","φ=x²+y²+C.",
"Is the gradient of a differentiable loss field conservative?","Locally, wherever a well-defined smooth scalar loss exists, its gradient is conservative.",
"Zero curl only implies a global potential under suitable domain assumptions (e.g. simply connected).");
add("double-integrals","Double integrals & area",3,"Intermediate","ipu",
"A double integral accumulates a quantity over a region. Under a constant density it is simply density times area.",
"∬ᴿf(x,y)dA ; area(R)=∬ᴿ1dA",
"Integrate f=1 over [0,2]×[0,3].","2×3=6.",
"Evaluate ∫₀¹∫₀² x dy dx.","∫₀¹ 2x dx=1.",
"How is expectation related to integration?","Continuous expectation integrates a value weighted by its probability density over a domain.",
"Changing integration order may require changing region bounds.");
add("triple-integrals","Triple integrals & volume",3,"Intermediate","ipu",
"Triple integration adds values throughout a 3D body. Coordinate transformations may require a Jacobian factor for volume distortion.",
"∭ⱽf dV ; volume(V)=∭ⱽ1 dV",
"Find the volume of [0,2]×[0,3]×[0,4].","24.",
"Integrate 1 over the unit cube.","1.",
"What does integrating a probability density over all of ℝ³ yield?","1 when it is a normalized probability density.",
"Remember the r or r² sinφ Jacobian in cylindrical/spherical coordinates.");
add("surface-integrals","Surface integrals & flux",3,"Advanced","ipu",
"Surface integrals measure quantities spread across a surface. Flux counts how strongly a vector field crosses the surface normal.",
"flux=∬ˢF·n dS",
"For constant F=(0,0,2), upward unit square, flux?","F·n=2, area=1, so flux=2.",
"Find flux of F=(0,0,3) through horizontal area 4 with upward normal.","12.",
"How might flux relate to conservation PDEs used in scientific ML?","Flux through the boundary controls change of a conserved quantity inside a volume.",
"Surface orientation changes the sign of flux.");
add("greens-theorem","Green's theorem",3,"Advanced","ipu",
"Green's theorem turns circulation around a closed planar curve into the curl accumulated inside it. Boundary behavior summarizes interior rotation.",
"∮꜀ P dx+Q dy=∬ᴿ(∂Q/∂x−∂P/∂y)dA",
"For P=−y/2,Q=x/2 on unit square, find circulation.","Integrand=1, so circulation=area=1 (counterclockwise).",
"State Green's theorem conditions in words.","Smooth enough P,Q on a region with appropriately oriented simple closed boundary.",
"Why are integral theorems useful for physics-informed ML?","They supply weak/integral forms of differential constraints, useful when derivatives are noisy.",
"Orientation and holes in the domain matter.");
add("stokes-theorem","Stokes' theorem",3,"Advanced","ipu",
"Stokes generalizes Green: circulation along a boundary equals curl flux through any spanning surface.",
"∮∂ˢF·dr = ∬ˢ(∇×F)·n dS",
"If curl F=(0,0,2) over upward unit disk, find circulation.","2π, since area of unit disk is π.",
"State Stokes' theorem in words.","The line integral around an oriented boundary equals the surface integral of curl with matching orientation.",
"How might curl constraints help train a physically consistent vector-field model?","Penalizing or constraining curl helps encode rotational behavior required by governing equations.",
"The right-hand rule couples curve orientation and surface normal.");
add("divergence-theorem","Gauss divergence theorem",3,"Advanced","ipu",
"The divergence theorem equates total outward flow through a closed surface with all local sources inside its volume.",
"∯∂ⱽF·n dS = ∭ⱽ∇·F dV",
"For F=(x,y,z) on a unit cube, find outward flux.","Divergence=3, volume=1, flux=3.",
"If div F=0 throughout a closed volume, what is net outward flux?","Zero under the theorem's smoothness assumptions.",
"How is this used in conservation-law neural solvers?","A model can be checked or trained against mass/energy balance over regions rather than only pointwise values.",
"This theorem applies to a closed boundary with outward orientation.");

// DEEP LEARNING BRIDGE
add("tensors-shapes","Tensors, axes & shapes",4,"Beginner","dl",
"A tensor generalizes scalar/vector/matrix arrays to more axes. Treating axes as named semantic dimensions prevents silent bugs in deep-learning code.",
"X ∈ ℝᴮˣᵀˣᴰ = batch × sequence × embedding",
"What is the shape of 16 sequences of 32 tokens with 64-dimensional embeddings?","(16,32,64).",
"Write the shape of a batch of 8 RGB 64×64 images in NCHW.","(8,3,64,64).",
"Why do attention layers need explicit head and sequence axes?","They calculate per-head token interactions; mixing axes silently changes the mathematical operation.",
"Shape compatibility is more important than variable names.");
add("broadcasting-einsum","Broadcasting, batches & einsum",4,"Intermediate","dl",
"Broadcasting repeats compatible dimensions without manually storing repeats. Einstein notation makes summed axes explicit.",
"Y[b,o]=Σᵢ X[b,i] W[i,o]",
"X shape (4,3), W shape (3,2): output?","(4,2).",
"What is the output shape of (5,1,3)+(1,4,3) under broadcasting?","(5,4,3).",
"Translate y=WX for a batch into index notation.","Y[b,o]=ΣᵢX[b,i]W[i,o].",
"Broadcastable shapes align from the right; matching non-unit sizes must agree.");
add("matrix-calculus","Matrix calculus & gradients",4,"Advanced","dl",
"A gradient of a scalar loss with respect to a matrix has the same shape as that matrix. Matrix calculus turns entire neural layers into short derivative expressions.",
"y=Wx ; L=½‖y−t‖² ⇒ ∂L/∂W=(y−t)xᵀ",
"For x=(2,1), error e=3, find gradient for a one-output row W.","∇ᵂL=e xᵀ=(6,3).",
"Differentiate f(w)=½‖w‖².","∇f=w.",
"Why is the gradient of Wx an outer product in a single-sample layer?","Each weight Wᵢⱼ affects output i in proportion to input xⱼ, so ∂L/∂Wᵢⱼ=δᵢxⱼ.",
"Track dimensions of derivatives; invalid shapes reveal chain-rule mistakes.");
add("jacobians-vjp","Jacobians, JVP & VJP",4,"Advanced","dl",
"The Jacobian is the best local linear approximation of a vector function. Reverse-mode backprop propagates vector–Jacobian products efficiently.",
"f:ℝⁿ→ℝᵐ ; Jᵢⱼ=∂fᵢ/∂xⱼ ; gₓ=Jᵀgᵧ",
"For f(x,y)=(x+y,xy), compute J at (2,3).","[[1,1],[3,2]].",
"Find Jacobian of f(x,y)=(x²,3y).","[[2x,0],[0,3]].",
"Why is reverse-mode AD favored for one scalar loss with millions of weights?","One reverse pass computes gradients of that scalar with respect to many inputs at a cost comparable to several forward evaluations.",
"Jacobian orientation conventions differ; state your layout.");
add("computational-graphs","Computational graphs & chain rule",4,"Intermediate","dl",
"A computation graph breaks complicated functions into elementary steps. Gradients flow backwards via the chain rule, summing contributions where paths merge.",
"dz/dx = (dz/dy)(dy/dx)",
"For z=(2x+1)², find dz/dx at x=1.","4(2x+1)=12.",
"Differentiate (x²+1)³.","6x(x²+1)².",
"Why do gradients from multiple branches add?","Total derivative collects every path through which the variable influences the final scalar loss.",
"Local derivatives multiply along paths and add across paths.");
add("backprop-from-scratch","Backpropagation, step by step",4,"Advanced","dl",
"Backprop is simply the chain rule organized in reverse. For a neuron: form a weighted sum, apply activation, compute loss, then propagate derivatives to every weight.",
"z=w·x+b; a=σ(z); L=½(a−y)²; ∂L/∂w=(a−y)a(1−a)x",
"For x=1,w=0,b=0,y=1, compute ∂L/∂w.","a=0.5; ∂L/∂w=(−0.5)(0.25)(1)=−0.125.",
"For σ(0)=0.5 and squared error target 1, find dL/dz.","(0.5−1)×0.5×0.5=−0.125.",
"Why do we multiply the activation derivative during backprop?","Because weights affect loss through z→a→L; the chain rule multiplies each local sensitivity.",
"Backprop is not a separate optimizer — it calculates gradients that an optimizer uses.");
add("activations","Activation functions & saturation",4,"Intermediate","dl",
"Nonlinear activations let stacked affine layers represent nonlinear functions. Derivatives determine whether useful gradients survive through many layers.",
"ReLU(z)=max(0,z) ; σ′(z)=σ(z)(1−σ(z))",
"Find ReLU(−3), ReLU(4).","0 and 4.",
"Compute σ′(0) for logistic sigmoid.","0.25.",
"Why do saturated sigmoids cause vanishing gradients?","For large |z|, σ′(z) approaches zero, reducing gradients through repeated chain-rule products.",
"The derivative of ReLU at zero requires a chosen subgradient convention.");
add("softmax-loss","Softmax, log-sum-exp & cross entropy",4,"Advanced","dl",
"Softmax turns logits into a distribution. Cross entropy compares that distribution with a target, and the combined derivative simplifies beautifully to prediction minus target.",
"pᵢ=exp(zᵢ)/Σⱼexp(zⱼ); ∂L/∂z=p−y",
"For logits (0,0), what are softmax probabilities?","(0.5,0.5).",
"If predicted p=(0.7,0.3), target y=(1,0), find p−y.","(−0.3,0.3).",
"Why subtract max(logits) before exponentiating?","Softmax is invariant to adding a constant; shifting prevents numerical overflow.",
"Never exponentiate very large logits without stabilization.");
add("gradient-descent","Gradient descent, SGD & learning rates",4,"Intermediate","dl",
"Gradient descent repeatedly follows a negative local slope. The learning rate sets step size; curvature determines which rates converge or oscillate.",
"θₖ₊₁=θₖ−η∇L(θₖ)",
"For L(w)=w², w=2, η=0.1, do one step.","Gradient=4; new w=2−0.1×4=1.6.",
"Minimize f(x)=x² for one step from x=3 at η=0.1.","x'=3−0.1(6)=2.4.",
"What happens on L(w)=w² when η>1?","Update w'=(1−2η)w has magnitude multiplier >1, so iterates diverge for nonzero w.",
"Large learning rate can overshoot even when gradients are accurate.");
add("embedding-geometry","Embeddings, similarity & retrieval",4,"Intermediate","dl",
"Embeddings represent words, images or users as learned coordinates. Dot products, cosine similarity, nearest neighbors and low-rank structures connect retrieval to linear algebra.",
"cos(u,v)=(u·v)/(‖u‖‖v‖)",
"For u=(1,0) and v=(0,2), calculate cosine similarity.","0; they are perpendicular.",
"Find cosine similarity of (1,1) and (2,2).","1, since the vectors are parallel in the same direction.",
"Why might normalized embeddings improve vector search?","The ranking focuses on angle rather than vector magnitude when cosine similarity is desired.",
"Similarity is model- and normalization-dependent; it need not be semantic truth.");
add("attention-matrices","Attention as matrix algebra",4,"Advanced","dl",
"Each token produces a query, key and value. Dot products produce token-to-token similarity; softmax weights values to create context-aware mixtures.",
"Attention(Q,K,V)=softmax(QKᵀ/√dₖ)V",
"If sequence length is 4, what is the attention score shape for one head?","4×4, one score for every query–key pair.",
"If Q is (5,8) and K is (7,8), what is QKᵀ?","(5,7), scores for 5 queries against 7 keys.",
"Why divide by √dₖ?","Without scaling, dot-product variance often grows with key dimension, pushing softmax toward saturation.",
"Attention weights sum to 1 across keys for each query, but are not necessarily symmetric.");
add("normalization","Normalization & gradient flow",4,"Advanced","dl",
"Normalization recenters and rescales activations. It changes the geometry of optimization and introduces nontrivial dependencies across feature coordinates.",
"LayerNorm(x)=(x−μ)/√(σ²+ε) · γ+β",
"For x=(1,3), find mean and population variance.","Mean=2; variance=((−1)²+1²)/2=1.",
"Standardize (2,4) using mean 3 and std 1.","(−1,1).",
"Why is epsilon included in the denominator?","It prevents division by zero and helps numerical stability.",
"BatchNorm and LayerNorm normalize over different axes.");
add("low-rank-compression","Low-rank factorization & LoRA",4,"Advanced","dl",
"A large weight update often can be represented using smaller factors. LoRA learns ΔW=BA with rank at most r instead of optimizing every entry of W.",
"ΔW=B A ; B∈ℝᵐˣʳ, A∈ℝʳˣⁿ ; rank(ΔW)≤r",
"For W of shape 1000×1000 and rank 8, compare parameter counts.","Full update has 1,000,000 values; factors have 16,000 (1000×8+8×1000).",
"For m=100,n=50,r=4, how many LoRA factor parameters?","4(100+50)=600 versus 5000 dense values.",
"Why can a low-rank adapter be efficient for fine-tuning?","It learns a restricted update subspace with far fewer trainable parameters.",
"Actual memory and compute depend on implementation; LoRA reduces trainable parameters, not base-model size.");

// RESEARCH
add("hessian-curvature","Hessian spectra & loss landscapes",5,"Research","research",
"The Hessian collects second derivatives and describes local curvature. Eigenvalue directions separate flat from sharp curvature and help analyze optimization behavior.",
"Hᵢⱼ=∂²L/(∂θᵢ∂θⱼ) ; vᵀHv measures directional curvature",
"For L(x,y)=x²+4y², find Hessian eigenvalues.","H=diag(2,8), eigenvalues 2 and 8.",
"Find Hessian of x²+y².","diag(2,2).",
"Why does large Hessian spectral norm constrain a stable gradient-descent step?","Large curvature can cause steps to overshoot even when low-curvature directions still move slowly.",
"Local Hessian curvature is not a complete measure of generalization.","Research task: numerically estimate the top Hessian eigenvalue of a tiny PyTorch model using Hessian–vector products; compare train stages.");
add("automatic-diff","Forward- and reverse-mode autodiff",5,"Research","research",
"Automatic differentiation is exact application of chain rules to program operations, up to floating-point error. JVPs propagate tangents; VJPs propagate cotangents.",
"JVP=Jv ; VJP=Jᵀu",
"For f(x)=x³ at x=2, JVP in direction v=4?","f′(2)=12; JVP=48.",
"For f(x)=x² at x=3, tangent v=2, compute JVP.","2xv=12.",
"When would forward mode be more efficient than reverse mode?","When few inputs feed many outputs; reverse mode shines when few outputs depend on many inputs.",
"Finite differences estimate derivatives; autodiff uses chain rules on the computational graph.","Research task: compare finite-difference gradients, torch.func.jvp and torch.autograd.grad on a small network.");
add("kronecker-products","Kronecker products & structured matrices",5,"Research","research",
"The Kronecker product builds large structured matrices from smaller ones. It appears in vectorization identities and approximate second-order optimization.",
"vec(AXB)=(Bᵀ⊗A)vec(X)",
"For A=[2] and B=[[1,2],[3,4]], find A⊗B.","[[2,4],[6,8]].",
"What is the shape of A⊗B if A is 2×3 and B is 4×5?","8×15.",
"Why approximate neural-network curvature with Kronecker factors?","Structure can reduce storage and inversion cost compared with a full Hessian.",
"Kronecker multiplication is not ordinary matrix multiplication.","Research task: construct the vectorization identity numerically and measure its floating-point error.");
add("spectral-graphs","Graph Laplacians & spectral learning",5,"Research","research",
"A graph Laplacian encodes connectivity and smoothness. Its smallest eigenvectors reveal clusters and low-frequency structure used in graph learning.",
"L=D−A ; xᵀLx=Σ_edges (xᵢ−xⱼ)² for undirected unweighted graphs",
"For two nodes joined by one edge, write L.","[[1,−1],[−1,1]], with eigenvalues 0 and 2.",
"What does the all-ones vector satisfy for L=D−A?","L1=0 for any graph with row sums defined in this convention.",
"Why are low-eigenvalue Laplacian modes called smooth?","Adjacent nodes take similar values, so the edge-difference energy is small.",
"The exact energy sum factor depends on whether you count directed or undirected edges.","Research task: compute graph Laplacian eigenmaps for a synthetic two-cluster graph and evaluate cluster separation.");
add("neural-tangent-kernel","Neural tangent kernel intuition",5,"Research","research",
"The NTK describes how parameter gradients induce an effective kernel between inputs. In certain wide-network limits it can approximate training dynamics.",
"K(x,x′)=∇θf(x;θ) · ∇θf(x′;θ)",
"If gradients for two inputs are (1,2) and (3,4), what is their NTK entry?","1×3+2×4=11.",
"Is the Gram matrix of parameter gradients positive semidefinite?","Yes; it is J Jᵀ.",
"How could the NTK spectrum influence training rates?","Different kernel eigenmodes can decay at different speeds under gradient flow.",
"Finite-width networks need not stay in the same kernel regime throughout training.","Research task: measure empirical NTK for tiny MLPs of increasing width and compare change during training.");
add("implicit-differentiation","Implicit layers & differentiating solutions",5,"Research","research",
"Some models define outputs as solutions of equations, not explicit layer formulas. Implicit differentiation differentiates the constraint instead of unrolling every solver step.",
"F(z,θ)=0 ⇒ dz/dθ=−(∂F/∂z)⁻¹(∂F/∂θ)",
"For z²−θ=0, z>0, find dz/dθ.","1/(2√θ), matching implicit formula.",
"For z+θ=0, find dz/dθ.","−1.",
"Why use implicit gradients for equilibrium models?","They can avoid retaining every forward iteration, given a suitable linear solve.",
"Requires nonsingular ∂F/∂z near the solution for the stated formula.","Research task: compare unrolled and implicit gradients for a fixed-point layer.");
add("randomized-svd","Randomized SVD & scalable approximation",5,"Research","research",
"Random projections identify a small subspace containing most matrix energy, then solve a much smaller SVD. This makes decomposition practical for enormous data.",
"Y=AΩ ; Q=orth(Y) ; B=QᵀA",
"If A is 10000×1000 and Ω is 1000×20, find Y shape.","10000×20.",
"What is the shape of QᵀA when Q is m×r and A is m×n?","r×n.",
"Why use randomized SVD to inspect transformer weights?","It estimates dominant singular directions with fewer passes and smaller dense decompositions.",
"Approximation quality depends on spectral decay, oversampling and iteration count.","Research task: benchmark randomized vs exact SVD on synthetic low-rank-plus-noise matrices.");
add("stability-gradients","Gradient stability, spectral norms & residuals",5,"Research","research",
"Deep networks compose Jacobians. Repeated singular-value expansion or contraction can explode or vanish gradients; residual paths provide shorter derivative routes.",
"∂hₙ/∂h₀ = JₙJₙ₋₁⋯J₁",
"If each layer scales by 0.5, what is scaling after 8 layers?","0.5⁸=1/256.",
"If 10 linear layers each scale by 1.1, total scaling?","1.1¹⁰≈2.59.",
"Why can residual connections improve gradient flow?","Their Jacobians include an identity term, creating a direct path for gradient propagation.",
"Spectral norm bounds are useful but not the whole story for nonlinear models.","Research task: plot gradient norms by depth for MLPs with and without residual connections.");
add("information-geometry","Fisher information & natural gradients",5,"Research","research",
"Parameter space has a model-dependent geometry. Fisher information measures local sensitivity of predictive distributions; natural gradients rescale directions accordingly.",
"Δθ = −η F⁻¹∇L (when F invertible)",
"If F=diag(4,1) and gradient=(4,1), find F⁻¹g.","(1,1).",
"Find F⁻¹g for F=diag(2,8), g=(2,8).","(1,1).",
"How does a natural gradient differ from Euclidean gradient descent?","It accounts for local distributional geometry rather than using the same scale in every parameter direction.",
"Fisher matrices can be huge or singular; practical methods approximate or damp them.","Research task: compare diagonal-Fisher preconditioning with vanilla SGD on a simple classifier.");
